pack compute-financial-metabolism
generation control-1
provenance wiring model/token spend into the financial membrane, 2026-08-27
authority measured
note Compute was being spent entirely outside the ledger. financial_metabolism.py has shipped the whole path -- ProviderModelUsageObservation with input/cached/reasoning token fields, PriceEvidence, value_provider_usage, CostAllocation -- and nothing had ever called it. This pass wires it against real invoices, and in doing so kills a scale bottleneck I had introduced myself.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE BOTTLENECK, AND WHY IT WAS THE WRONG SHAPE OF ANSWER
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

claim clm:manual-rate-verification-does-not-scale
  status RETRACTED
  verification MEASURED
  original_claim I recorded gpt-5.6-terra usage at basis='unknown' with the reason "no published rate I can verify".
  why_wrong two separate errors. First, factually: gpt-5.6-terra IS in the OpenRouter published book at $2.00/M input and $12.00/M output. I had not looked. Second, structurally: even had it been absent, "a human verifies each rate" makes the ledger cost one lookup per model, so it stops at one model. That is a bottleneck presented as a result.
  correction rate resolution is a MECHANISM, not a lookup
  note the user identified this as a scale bottleneck before I did

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE CASCADE THAT REPLACED IT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

claim clm:rates-derived-from-billing
  status STANDING
  verification MEASURED
  claim Model rates fall out of provider billing EXACTLY, with no lookup and no human.
  method cost(model, line_item) / tokens(model, class), from OpenAI /organization/costs and /organization/usage/completions
  unlocked_by the OPEN_AI_ADMIN_API_KEY added mid-session; the ordinary key returns 403 missing scope api.usage.read
  window 30 days, $19.3810 billed, 5 models
  derived gpt-5.6-sol $3.814/M fresh in, $0.400/M cached, $20.000/M out
  derived gpt-5.4-2026-03-05 $2.500 / $0.250 / $15.000
  derived gpt-5-2025-08-07 $1.250 / $0.125 / $10.000
  derived gpt-4o-mini-2024-07-18 $0.155 / -- / $0.619
  derived gpt-4.1-mini-2025-04-14 $0.560 / -- / $2.240
  cross_validated three exact agreements against the published book -- gpt-5 $1.250 vs $1.250, gpt-5.4 $2.500 vs $2.500, gpt-4o-mini $0.155 vs $0.150
  contract_differs gpt-5.6-sol derives 1.91x list and gpt-4.1-mini 1.40x; that is real contract pricing, which is precisely what a published book cannot tell you
  consequence any model the organisation actually uses is priced automatically; a model never used has no rate, which is correct because it also cost nothing
  scales yes -- this is the answer to the bottleneck

claim clm:sibling-pricing-refused
  status REFUSED
  verification MEASURED
  claim Pricing an unknown model from its nearest published models in latent space does not work.
  method leave-one-out over all 389 priced models: hide one, price it from its k=5 latent neighbours, measure error
  result median error 59%, median 2.0x off, only 61.7% within 2x of true
  gate_sweep best spread gate <=5.0x still leaves 50% median error at 31% coverage
  worst gpt-oss-20b true $0.030 estimated $2.000 (67x); gpt-4o-2024-05-13 true $5.000 estimated $0.150 (33x)
  why model identity text recovers vendor FAMILY but not price TIER; rates inside one family vary by more than an order of magnitude and nothing in the name separates them
  decision refused outright rather than gated, because rungs 1 and 2 cover every real case, so a rung with 50% median error is unnecessary as well as dangerous
  note my auto-gate would have ADMITTED it at 73% within-2x; the gate criterion was too loose and I overrode it on the median error

claim clm:cascade-final-shape
  status STANDING
  verification MEASURED
  rung_1 DERIVED -- provider billing arithmetic. basis 'billed'. Exact, contract-accurate, scales with usage.
  rung_2 PUBLISHED -- price book by exact model id, 415 models. basis 'billed', because a published tariff is the seller's own statement.
  rung_3 REFUSED -- latent siblings, see clm:sibling-pricing-refused
  rung_4 UNKNOWN -- basis 'unknown' with a reason. Now genuinely the exception.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TOKEN REDUCTION, MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSUMED
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

claim clm:prompt-cache-is-39-percent-of-spend
  status STANDING
  verification MEASURED
  claim Prompt caching is the highest-leverage token-reduction lever currently measurable, and its value is now a billed quantity rather than an estimate.
  evidence 5,100,942 of 8,759,091 input tokens served cached (58.2%)
  evidence cached input bills at roughly a tenth of fresh input on every model observed -- gpt-5.6-sol $0.400 vs $3.814, gpt-5 $0.125 vs $1.250, gpt-5.4 $0.250 vs $2.500
  evidence caching avoided $7.4721 of an otherwise-$26.85 bill: 39% of actual spend
  method derived from billed line items, which separate "cached input" from "input"; no assumption involved
  cross_check codex session rollouts on disk show the same shape independently -- 35M input tokens across sessions with 33M cached, one session at 97.5%

claim clm:commons-cannot-answer-the-token-question
  status STANDING
  verification MEASURED
  claim The Partner Commons cannot currently answer "which methods reduce token use", and the reason is the coverage gap.
  evidence latent query for token-reduction returns Weights & Biases, DVC, JSON Schema, ArcticDB, MLflow, FOCUS 1.4 -- lifecycle and billing profiles, not compression methods
  cause every actual token-reduction method is among the 44 missing from the 112 index: BitNet, GigaToken, Zstd, llama.cpp/GGUF, ggml, ONNX Runtime, Feldera/DBSP
  consequence the coverage gap is not cosmetic; it makes the commons unable to serve the query the user actually has
  action loading the outside-112 groups is now the blocking dependency for Program I and Program K

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THE LEDGER NOW HOLDS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

upgrade upg:compute-ledger-live
  status LANDED
  verification MEASURED
  state ~/.bonfyre/compute-ledger.sqlite3
  content 13 usage occurrences and 13 economic measures at basis='billed'
  first this is the first compute cost the financial membrane has ever metabolised
  discipline usage is recorded per token CLASS with only that class's dimension carried, so a fresh-input rate can never be applied to a cached-token count
  guards_that_fired the module refused a replayed attempt_ref with changed usage evidence, and refused a price whose evidence_ref did not match the usage attempt's pricing_evidence_ref -- both were my bugs and both were caught by the module rather than by me

upgrade upg:providers-as-foreign-twins
  status LANDED
  verification MEASURED
  claim Eight model/compute providers are now observed rather than merely configured.
  live google-ai-studio 50 models, openai 124, mistral 54, cohere 20, groq 14, openrouter, huggingface, firecrawl -- all HTTP 200
  rights OBSERVE only; no BIND, no ACT
  law external project is not a semantic owner

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# FINDINGS THAT NEED A HUMAN
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

claim clm:budget-membrane-contradictory-and-unenforced
  status UNRESOLVED
  verification REPRODUCED
  finding provider.refs.env declares the spend membrane TWICE with different values
  conflict MAX_DAILY_SPEND_USD = 25 and 75
  conflict MAX_MODEL_CALLS_PER_RUN = 2000 and 6000
  conflict MAX_CRAWL_PAGES_PER_RUN = 15000 and 5000
  conflict MIN_ACTIONABILITY_SCORE = 0.68 and 0.72
  effect shell sourcing is last-wins and silent, so the effective budget depends on line order rather than on a decision
  worse nothing reads these values at all -- no control-plane module enforces a spend cap, so the membrane is declarative only
  action_required pick single values, then bind them to the ledger that now exists

claim clm:firecrawl-overdrawn
  status UNRESOLVED
  verification REPRODUCED
  finding firecrawl remaining_credits = -3 against plan_credits = 1000, billing period ending 2026-09-16
  recorded as a FinancialPosition at basis='accrued' in CREDIT currency
  action_required top up or accept degraded crawl capability

claim clm:openai-key-scope-gap
  status RESOLVED
  verification MEASURED
  finding the ordinary OPENAI_API_KEY returns 403 on /organization/costs and /organization/usage: missing scope api.usage.read
  resolution the user added OPEN_AI_ADMIN_API_KEY mid-session, which unlocked both endpoints and made rung 1 possible
  note the entire derived-rate result depends on that key; without it the cascade degrades to rung 2 only
